expectations of a giant increase in  thanksgiving travel this year  and fears that there will be chaos at  airports aaa is predicting that more  than 53 million people will be driving  boarding a train or bus or yes flying  this holiday and air travel is expected  to be up 80 percent compared to last  year returning to near pre-pandemic  levels all that comes at a difficult  time for the industry airline industry  employees are not where they need to be  there are widespread staff shortages and  all federal employees which includes tsa  staff must be fully vaccinated by next  monday as of last month 40 percent of  tsa employees were not vaccinated or had  not submitted their vaccination status  so we are joined now by tsa  administrator david pakuski who oversees  security at nearly 440 airports in this  fine country he joins us now from  laguardia airport where i am certain he  did not have trouble with tsa  good morning mr administrator  let's start with the big picture people  are going to be flocking to airports in  the next couple of weeks what should  they expect  well they should expect that they will  we will meet our throughput standards i  mean if they're a standard lane  passenger it's going to take about 30  minutes if they're a pre-check passenger  10 minutes or less and i don't think  they should expect chaos we work very  closely with the airlines with the  airports we have travel projections for  the coming week and we're very confident  this is going to be a very smooth  operation uh over the next several days  i'm glad to hear you're confident the  reason people were wondering if maybe it  was going to be a problem is the the  mandate requiring vaccination among  federal employees and it seemed like a  big number of your employees were not  there yet has the number shifted what's  going on there  oh the number's improved greatly and i  don't expect the vaccine mandate to have  any impact whatsoever on thanksgiving  travel  and the good news for travelers is that  more and more people including tsa  employees are now vaccinated so from a  public health perspective it's a much  safer experience  will employees your tsa employees lose  their job if they're not vaccinated  well we will go through a progressive  discipline process with our employees  who have not been vaccinated but we have  a lot of employees who've asked for  either medical or religious exemptions  as well and so we'll go through the  process of looking at each one of those  individual cases and make a  determination over the next several  weeks so that yes there is the potential  if you  do not have an approved exemption and  you are not vaccinated that you will  lose your job because it's very  important that we have our workforce  fully vaccinated  you know another issue is  it's very disturbing issue scary i think  the outburst of passion of unruly  passengers it's actually gotten very  physical and very very dangerous how is  it affecting you and your team and what  are you guys doing about that  no you're right i mean you know the the  level of unruly passengers has gotten  very high uh and i've been the tsa  administrator for a little over four  years i have never seen it at these  levels what we're doing about it is  we're working very closely with the  carriers  the flight attendants the flight deck  crews the airports and the faa to do  everything that we can to message how  dangerous this behavior is what the  penalties are if you engage in it  and we're also working hard with with  flight attendants to provide  self-defense training and de-escalation  training for them and i would just say  at this point that you know really when  i look at this and i see these incidents  occurring  the flood attendants deserve enormous  credit uh for a very hard job to begin  with and then what they're dealing with  now uh in the year i mean it seems like  nearly every day there's a level of an  in-flight disturbance that gets beyond  the verbal argument and and gets into  some level of physical contact which is  something like i said that we hadn't  seen  in the past certainly not in my  experience as the administrator  david i want to stick with those unruly  passengers for a second whether it's  before uh during or even after the  flight you know as a parent the  knee-jerk reaction is to get involved  i'm traveling with my kids or my wife  and sometimes these workers seem a  little bit overwhelmed what would be  your advice to somebody that is sitting  back watching something going on and  thinking to themselves i should get  involved because the the people working  might need help  well i think the best advice i could  give is to try to de-escalate the  situation um and and that's part of the  trend that we provide uh and and if the  flight attendant asks for assistance uh  then to provide them the assistance that  they ask for i mean it it's hard  particularly hard if there's more than  one passenger involved in creating the  disturbance and oftentimes we see that  sometimes it's it's two passengers  seeing next to each other or a passenger  in front of another that you know  reclines the seat for example so things  that in the past were minor issues  seemed to be much more significant  issues to cause you know a physical  confrontation when it didn't happen that  way in the past generally  with these flights increasing and  holiday travel going up this information  is valuable david prakovsky thank you so  much we appreciate you joining us this  morning
 

